// COMPARISON BINDS TIGHTER THAN EQUALITY — `a < b == c > d` is
// `(a<b) == (c>d)`, comparing two bools, and `1 + 1 == 2` is `(1+1) == 2`,
// never `1 + (1==2)`.
//
// This is the line where the wrong tier is most tempting to build: `==`
// and `<` look like one family of "comparison" operators, and a table that
// puts them on one row reads `1 < 2 == 4 > 3` as `((1 < (2==4)) > 3)` —
// bool against integer, which cannot even type-check. That is why this
// file asserts OUTPUTS rather than acceptance: every line must RUN and
// print a specific value, so the broken-table world fails this corpus
// entry twice over — it rejects, or it prints something else. The
// additive line is the sneaky half: `1 + 1 == 2` compiles under either
// tier assignment when the language coerces, and only prints differently
// (`true` vs `1`) in the wrong world. Zen prints its bools spelled out,
// which is what makes the difference visible at all.
//
// THE ONE-LINE BREAK: delete the separate equality row — move `==`/`!=`
// onto PREC.comparison (merges the tiers, `cmpcmp` stops compiling) or
// onto PREC.additive (`add_eq` reads `1 + (1==2)` and prints 1, not
// true). Either single edit reddens this test.

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    println("add_eq {}", 1 + 1 == 2);

    // (1<2) and (4>3), both true, compared equal.
    println("cmpcmp {}", 1 < 2 == 4 > 3);
    println("not_cmp {}", !(3 < 1));

    // the explicit-paren twin of cmpcmp's left half: parens around a
    // comparison that already outbinds == are noise, and the answer does
    // not move.
    println("paren {}", (1 < 2) == true);
    Ok(0);
}
